<h2 id="belt-heading">Trough Conveyor Belt Types</h2>
<h3>Fabric-Reinforced Belts</h3>
<p>Multi-ply fabric belts use layers of polyester (EP), nylon (NN), or polyester-nylon (EP/NN) fabric for tensile reinforcement. Standard belt ratings range from 150 to 800 PIW (pounds per inch of width). Fabric belts suit conveyor lengths up to approximately 3,000 feet depending on load and incline. They cost less than steel-cord belts and are easier to splice in the field. Continental, Fenner Dunlop, and Goodyear (now Veyance) manufacture fabric conveyor belts.</p>
<h3>Steel-Cord Reinforced Belts</h3>
<p>Steel-cord belts embed parallel steel cables in the belt carcass for extreme tensile strength. Ratings range from 1,000 to over 7,000 PIW. Steel-cord belts serve long-distance overland conveyors, high-tonnage <a href="/industries/mining-conveyors/">mining conveyors</a>, and applications where belt stretch must be minimized. They require vulcanized splicing with specialized equipment. Bridgestone, Continental, and Fenner Dunlop manufacture steel-cord conveyor belts.</p>
<h3>Cover Grades</h3>
<p>The belt cover (top and bottom rubber layers) protects the reinforcement from abrasion, impact, heat, oil, and chemical attack. CEMA/RMA grades include: Grade I for severely abrasive conditions (mining, aggregate), Grade II for moderately abrasive conditions (grain, fertilizer, cement), and specialty grades for oil-resistant, fire-resistant, and heat-resistant applications. Cover thickness ranges from 1/16" to 1/2" depending on application severity.</p>
<h3>Belt Flexibility</h3>
<p>Troughing requires the belt to flex transversely. Stiffer belts resist troughing and do not conform well to the idler profile, reducing effective carrying capacity and causing edge spillage. The belt must be flexible enough for the specified troughing angle. Higher troughing angles (45°) require more flexible belts than lower angles (20°). Belt manufacturers publish minimum recommended troughing angles for each belt construction.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Trough Conveyor Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Mining and aggregate:</strong> Trough conveyors move ore, overburden, crushed stone, sand, and gravel from extraction points to processing plants. Belt widths from 36" to 96" handle throughputs from hundreds to thousands of tons per hour. Overland conveyors replace haul trucks for distances exceeding one mile, reducing fuel, tire, and road maintenance costs.</p>
<p><strong>Grain and agriculture:</strong> Troughed belts transport grain from receiving pits to storage silos and from silos to processing or loadout. The enclosed trough reduces grain loss from wind and edge spillage. Food-grade belt covers prevent contamination. Inclined trough conveyors use <a href="/conveyor-types/cleated-conveyors/">cleated belts</a> or chevron-patterned covers for steep angles.</p>
<p><strong>Cement and concrete:</strong> Trough conveyors move limestone, clinker, sand, and finished cement from quarry to kiln to silo to loadout. High-temperature belt covers handle hot clinker from the kiln. Dust-control hoods and enclosed galleries minimize fugitive dust emissions. Martin Engineering and Flexco provide belt cleaning and dust-suppression accessories for cement trough conveyors.</p>
<p><strong>Power generation:</strong> Coal-fired power plants use trough conveyors to transport coal from rail unloading facilities or barge docks to boiler feed silos. Typical capacities: 500-5,000 tons per hour on 48" to 72" wide belts. Fire-resistant belt covers and fire detection systems are required for coal handling per NFPA standards.</p>
<p><strong>Packaging bulk materials:</strong> In <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging line</a> applications, smaller trough conveyors (18"-36" wide) transport bulk ingredients from storage to filling machines. Sugar, salt, flour, pet food, fertilizer, and chemical powders move through troughed belt systems that feed weighing and bagging equipment.</p>
<p><strong>Waste and recycling:</strong> Trough conveyors transport municipal solid waste, construction debris, and recyclables from tipping floors to sorting stations. Wide belts (48"-72") handle the variable density and size of waste streams. Impact idlers at loading points absorb the shock of heavy items dropped from loaders.</p>
<h2 id="design-heading">Design Considerations</h2>
<h3>Loading &amp; Transfer Points</h3>
<p>Material loading is the highest-stress point on a trough conveyor. Proper chute design directs material onto the belt center at a velocity matching the belt speed. Impact idlers absorb drop energy. Skirt boards contain material during the settling zone. Poor loading causes belt mistracking, premature wear, and fugitive material spillage. CEMA's belt conveyor design manual (7th Edition) provides detailed loading point engineering guidance.</p>
<h3>Belt Cleaning</h3>
<p>Material carry-back on the belt return side causes buildup on return idlers, tracking problems, and housekeeping issues. Primary belt cleaners mount at the head pulley discharge point. Secondary cleaners mount behind the head pulley. Martin Engineering, Flexco, and Brelko manufacture belt cleaner systems. Effective belt cleaning is especially critical for sticky materials like wet clay, damp coal, and processed foods.</p>
<h3>Conveyor Structure</h3>
<p>Trough conveyor support structures include ground-level stringers, elevated gallery frames, and pipe trusses. Structural design must account for belt tension, material weight, wind loading (for outdoor installations), seismic requirements, and maintenance access. Walkways and access platforms along the conveyor length are required for idler replacement, belt inspection, and safety per MSHA and OSHA standards.</p>
<h3>Drive Systems</h3>
<p>Trough conveyors use head-end, tail-end, or dual-pulley drive configurations depending on conveyor length and load. Short conveyors (under 500 ft) typically use a single head-end drive. Long conveyors use dual drives or intermediate booster drives. <a href="/resources/conveyor-motors-drives/">Variable frequency drives</a> (VFDs) enable soft starting, speed control, and energy savings at partial loads. Regenerative braking recovers energy on decline conveyors carrying heavy loads downhill.</p>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a trough conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A trough conveyor shapes a flat belt into a concave U-shape using angled idler rollers. This troughed profile increases carrying capacity 2-3x over a flat belt and prevents bulk material from spilling off the edges. It is the standard conveyor for mining, aggregate, grain, and cement handling.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is a troughing idler?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A troughing idler is a set of rollers arranged in a V or U shape. Standard three-roll idlers have one flat center roller and two angled wing rollers at 20°, 35°, or 45°. Five-roll idlers add extra wing rollers for deeper troughs on wide belts. Impact, training, and return idlers serve specialized support functions.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">Trough conveyor vs. flat belt conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Trough conveyors shape the belt into a U-profile for 2-3x more bulk material capacity. Flat belts stay horizontal on a slider bed for packaged goods, boxes, and unit loads. Trough conveyors dominate bulk handling (ore, grain, sand). Flat belts dominate <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging</a> and distribution.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How far can a trough conveyor transport material?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>From a few hundred feet to over 10 miles in a single flight. Long-distance overland conveyors use steel-cord reinforced belts and can replace haul trucks in mining. Practical length depends on belt tension rating, drive power, material weight, terrain, and whether horizontal curves are required.</p>

</div>
</div>
